    {
      "path": "server log",
      "summary": "Show the SQL Server error log's latest entries, or the Agent log's, filtered by text and time",
      "description": "One row per log entry, oldest first, read by xp_readerrorlog, which filters on the server. --search keeps the entries whose text holds the string, case-insensitively; given twice, both (AND, xp_readerrorlog's own meaning, not OR). --since keeps the entries from that moment on, YYYY-MM-DD[THH:MM[:SS]], read as the server's local time: the log records no time zone, so a Z or an offset is refused (exit 2), and date is shown the same way, with no zone. --lines keeps the last that many of the matching entries (default 100, 0 for every one). --archive reads an older file (0, the default, is the current log; 1 the one before it), one file per run: an entry older than the file is not in it, so a --since before the file's start needs a higher --archive. --agent reads the SQL Server Agent log instead, its level column (1 error, 2 warning, 3 information) in place of process. A sysadmin reads through xp_readerrorlog itself; any other login through sp_readerrorlog, which needs securityadmin, VIEW SERVER STATE or (SQL Server 2022 on) VIEW ANY ERROR LOG — without one of them the command fails, exit 9 — and, the procedure taking no dates, the whole file is read before --since applies. An archive that does not exist is exit 8.",
