    {
      "path": "server traceflag on",
      "summary": "Turn trace flags on for every session, until the next restart",
      "description": "DBCC TRACEON for each flag. Needs --allow admin, and sysadmin on the server (without it: exit 9). Always global (DBCC TRACEON/TRACEOFF(flag, -1)): a session flag would die with this command's connection. Flags apply in the order given; the first the server refuses stops the run (exit 5), and the change set still shows what the others did. --dry-run prints the DBCC statements and a planned_change set (flag, current, new) without running them. Otherwise the change set holds before, requested and after, from DBCC TRACESTATUS read before and after. A flag is lost when the SQL Server service restarts; a lasting one is a startup parameter (-T, or mssql-conf traceflag on Linux), which no SQL statement sets. The server does not refuse every flag it will not honour: a startup-only one (834) is skipped with no error, so a flag the second read does not show is a warning. A number the server knows no flag for is refused (Msg 2560; the highest accepted varies by version).",
