    {
      "path": "db set",
      "summary": "Change database options: recovery, compatibility level, read-only, Query Store, ALTER DATABASE SET switches",
      "description": "ALTER DATABASE … SET, one statement per --option KEY=VALUE, in the order given. Needs --allow admin, and ALTER on the database (without it: exit 9). Keys and values ignore case. RECOVERY takes FULL, SIMPLE or BULK_LOGGED; COMPATIBILITY_LEVEL a level from 100 up to the server's own, in steps of 10; READ_ONLY ON or OFF; QUERY_STORE ON (as READ_WRITE), READ_WRITE, READ_ONLY or OFF, keeping every other Query Store option as it is; PAGE_VERIFY CHECKSUM, TORN_PAGE_DETECTION or NONE; CURSOR_DEFAULT LOCAL or GLOBAL; CONTAINMENT NONE or PARTIAL; every other key ON or OFF. An unknown key or a value the key does not take is exit 2 before anything runs. The statements run one by one, outside any transaction (ALTER DATABASE cannot run in one): the first failure stops the run, the ones before it stay applied, and the change set shows them (exit 5). READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT, CONTAINMENT and READ_ONLY need the database to themselves: they wait for its other sessions to leave, up to --timeout (exit 6). --rollback-immediate disconnects those sessions instead, rolling back their open transactions, and needs --confirm with the database's name as given (exit 7 without). --dry-run prints the statements and a planned_change set (option, current, new) without running them; otherwise the change set holds before, requested and after, from sys.databases (and sys.database_query_store_options) read before and after. USER_ACCESS, OFFLINE and ONLINE are not offered.",
      "operationClass": "admin",
      "arguments": [
        {
          "name": "name",
          "help": "Database name",
          "format": null,
          "required": true,
