    {
      "path": "plan estimated",
      "summary": "Show the estimated execution plan of T-SQL without running it (SET SHOWPLAN_XML ON)",
      "description": "Compiles every batch under SET SHOWPLAN_XML ON on one session: the server returns each statement's plan and runs nothing, so no --allow is needed whatever the script does. Because nothing runs, a statement that uses a #temp table the script creates earlier fails to compile (Msg 208), as in SSMS. The login needs SHOWPLAN in the database (exit 9 without it) and rights on the objects. SET PARSEONLY, NOEXEC and SHOWPLAN_* in the script are refused (exit 7). --plan-format summary (default) answers sets statements (cost, rows, DOP, memory grant), operators (the --top costliest by own estimated cost across statements, cost_pct its share of its statement), missing_indexes (with a CREATE INDEX to review) and warnings; tree is every operator in preorder with its depth; xml is the plan document itself. --plan-file writes that document, as the query editor's Save Execution Plan As does (UTF-8), for SSMS or the plan viewer to open; an existing file needs --force. There is no --param: under SHOWPLAN_XML the server plans an sp_executesql call as the EXEC alone, not the statement inside (found live on 17); write the values into the script.",
      "operationClass": "read",
      "arguments": [],
      "options": [
        {
          "name": "--sql",
          "short": "-Q",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [],
          "default": null,
          "negation": null,
          "env": null,
          "help": "Inline T-SQL (fine for a short query)"
        },
        {
          "name": "--file",
          "short": "-i",
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [],
          "default": null,
