    {
      "path": "script object",
      "summary": "Script a table, view, procedure, function, trigger, sequence or synonym as CREATE, ALTER, DROP or a DML template",
      "description": "The object's Script As text, the same the TUI's Script <Object> as ▸ … ▸ File... writes: it opens with USE [database], then the statement. --as picks the verb; each kind offers only what Object Explorer does — every kind create, drop and drop-and-create; alter a view, procedure, function or trigger; select, insert, update and delete a table or view; execute a procedure; select a function. A verb the kind lacks is exit 2, naming the kind's verbs. A record — object, type, verb — and the script: text prints it under an === SCRIPT (n lines) === marker, line breaks kept, and --max-col-width does not cut it; JSON gives it as one string. A constraint, index or other object of a table is not scripted alone (its table's CREATE carries it), nor is an object SQL Server ships. Scripting reads definitions, which need VIEW DEFINITION: without it a module, or a table with a default, check, computed column or filtered index, is exit 9. An encrypted module has no definition to script: create, alter and drop-and-create are exit 11 (drop works). --script-file writes the script as File... does.",
