    {
      "path": "data profile",
      "summary": "Profile a table's or view's columns: nulls, distinct values, ranges, lengths",
      "description": "One aggregate SELECT over the object (several for more than 100 columns), with --where when given, answered as set columns: column, type, rows, nulls, null_pct, distinct, min, max, min_len, max_len. It reads every row --where keeps, and COUNT(DISTINCT) sorts or hashes each column: on a large table, narrow it with --columns or --where. What a column gets depends on its type (an alias type's base type): numbers, dates and times, strings (sysname too), binary, uniqueidentifier, sql_variant, hierarchyid and rowversion get distinct, min and max (bit's as 0/1); strings also get min_len/max_len in characters (LEN: trailing spaces not counted), binary in bytes. xml, text, ntext, image, geography, geometry, vector, CLR types and Always Encrypted columns get rows and nulls alone; the rest is NULL. A graph table's internal columns are left out. --top-values n adds set top_values (column, value, count): each column's n most frequent values, NULL among them, most frequent first, one SELECT … GROUP BY per column that has distinct. A record — object, type, tsql (the aggregate SELECTs, GO between) — then the sets.",
      "operationClass": "read",
      "arguments": [
