    {
      "path": "data import",
      "summary": "Load a .csv, .tsv or .jsonl file into a table (bulk copy)",
      "description": "Streams --in-file into the table through SQL Server's bulk copy, the way bcp and the Import wizard load. The file is what data export writes: .csv or .tsv with a header row (NULL an empty field, the empty string \"\"; a byte order mark is ignored), or gossms's .jsonl (a _resultSet line, one object per row, the _summary line ignored); one result set per file. --in-format names the format for stdin (--in-file -) or another extension. File columns are matched to the table's by name, exactly or else ignoring case; a name the table lacks is exit 2. The table's columns missing from the file take their defaults; computed, rowversion and GENERATED ALWAYS columns in the file are skipped with a warning, so an export loads back; an identity column in the file keeps the file's values. Each cell is converted to its column's type and checked before it is sent — range, length, scale, code page — and the first that fails is exit 2 naming the line and column. sql_variant, CLR (geography, geometry, hierarchyid) and Always Encrypted columns are exit 11 before loading. The load is one batch, all or nothing: on any failure the table is as it was. NULLs stay NULL (KEEPNULLS); CHECK and FOREIGN KEY constraints are checked unless --no-check-constraints; triggers fire only with --fire-triggers. Needs --allow write and INSERT on the table (exit 9 without). --truncate-first empties the table first (TRUNCATE TABLE): it is ddl, needs ALTER on the table, and needs --confirm with the table as given; it cannot share the load's transaction, so a load that then fails leaves the table empty. --dry-run reads and converts the whole file and loads nothing. A record — object, type, inFile, format, truncateFirst, rows, dryRun (and tsql, the TRUNCATE, on a dry run) — and set columns: each file column and the table column it loads (action load or skipped), then the table's columns left to their defaults (action default).",
